    "knowledge_point": "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position",
    "problem": "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=3{t}^{2}+2t,$ where $t$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=15$ when $t=2,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$",
    "worked_solution": "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(2)=15.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =(2)^{3}+(2)^{2}+C \\\\ 15 & =8+4+C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+3.\n$$",
